solnix: libld -- sort the output relocations on the output symbol index, not on the Sym_desc address, so that
identical inputs give identical objects.

SYMPTOM (wave6w/wave6x, 2026-11-05; /scratch/solnix/wave6/LD-NONDETERMINISM.md): rust-1.99.0's librustc_driver differed
between two builds only in .SUNW_reloc (same 229517 relocations, 7627 symbol groups in another order). Relinking the
saved, byte-identical inputs with the same store ld: dixi 3/3 identical, lixi 3/3 different (2-3 values per 8-16 runs).

MECHANISM: do_sorted_outrelocs() qsorts the non-PLT output relocations with reloc_compare() on (NEEDED index,
Sym_desc pointer, offset). The pointer is a libumem heap address, so the order of the symbol groups follows the
allocator: it differs between hosts and, on a many-CPU host, between runs.

THE CHANGE: key 2 is the output symbol table index the relocation is written with (the r_info symbol; for SECTION
symbols the output section symbol), as GNU ld's -z combreloc sort does (bfd elflink.c elf_link_sort_cmp1). RELATIVE and
REGISTER relocations have STN_UNDEF and stay first, grouped by type (DT_RELACOUNT). The relocations against each symbol
stay contiguous (one ld.so.1 lookup per group). A last tie-break on type makes the order total.

MEASURED (libld1, /scratch/solnix/libld1/): the librustc_driver relink with this libld gives one .SUNW_reloc on lixi
(12/12) and the same bytes on dixi (8/8); only .SUNW_reloc ever changes. elf-tests 23/3 with the new relocs/order test
(stock 22/4; the 3 are environment failures, the same with stock). NOT WIRED: it changes .SUNW_reloc in nearly every
linked object (jq 2/2, harfbuzz 7/8, openssl 6/7), so it lands with the next gate proto rebuild (see the .notes.md).

